<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-underdamped" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Behavior of Underdamped Solutions</title>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       OBSERVATION: SOLUTION FORM
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
<p>
  Recall from the previous page that we are in the underdamped case when
  <m>0 \lt c^2 \lt 4km</m>. Solving the characteristic equation
  <m>mr^2+cr+k=0</m> in this case gives complex roots <m>r = \alpha \pm \omega i</m>,
  which leads to the general solution
  <me>x(t) = e^{\alpha t}\bigl(c_1\cos(\omega t) + c_2\sin(\omega t)\bigr).</me>
  If you want to see exactly where <m>\alpha</m> and <m>\omega</m> come from, check
  out the dropdown below.
</p>

<justification>
  <title>Where do alpha and omega come from? (can skim)</title>
  <p>
    By the quadratic formula, the roots of <m>mr^2+cr+k=0</m> are
    <me>
      r=\frac{-c \pm \sqrt{c^2-4km}}{2m} = \frac{-c}{2m}\pm\frac{\sqrt{c^2-4km}}{2m}.
    </me>
    Since <m>c^2 \lt 4km</m>, the quantity under the square root is negative, so we can write
    <m>\sqrt{c^2-4km} = i\sqrt{4km-c^2}</m>. This means the roots are complex,
    <m>r = \alpha \pm \omega i</m>, where
    <me>\alpha = \frac{-c}{2m} \quad \text{and} \quad \omega = \frac{\sqrt{4km-c^2}}{2m}.</me>
    Note that <m>\alpha \lt 0</m>, since <m>c, m \gt 0</m> means <m>-c/(2m)</m> is always negative.
  </p>
</justification>
  <observation xml:id="obs-underdamped-form">
    <title>Underdamped Solution Form</title>
    <p>
      When <m>c^2 \lt 4km</m>, the characteristic equation has complex conjugate roots
      and the system is <term>underdamped</term>. The general solution takes the form:
      <me>x(t) = e^{\alpha t}\bigl(c_1 \cos(\omega t) + c_2 \sin(\omega t)\bigr),</me>
      where <m>\alpha </m> and <m>\omega</m> are determined by <m>m</m>, <m>c</m>, and <m>k</m>.

    
    </p>

    <p>
      And since <m>m</m>, <m>c</m>, and <m>k</m> are all positive, <m>\alpha</m> is always
      negative, so the factor <m>e^{\alpha t}</m> is always exponentially decaying.
    </p>
  </observation>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       PROSE: DECAYING ENVELOPE
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <p>
    Notice this solution has two pieces working together: an exponentially decaying
    factor <m>e^{\alpha t}</m> (with <m>\alpha \lt 0</m>) and an oscillating
    trig expression <m>c_1 \cos(\omega t) + c_2 \sin(\omega t)</m>. Think of the
    exponential as a shrinking envelope that squeezes the oscillations toward zero
    as time goes on.
  </p>

  <p>
    To see how <m>\alpha</m> affects the solution, the graph below compares the pure
    trig part (no decay) to the full underdamped solution. Move the slider to change
    the decay rate.
  </p>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       INTERACTIVE 1: COMPARE TRIG VS DAMPED TRIG
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <figure xml:id="fig-underdamped-compare">
    <caption>
      Left: the oscillating factor <m>3\sin(2t) + \cos(2t)</m> alone.
      Right: the full underdamped solution <m>e^{\alpha t}(3\sin(2t) + \cos(2t))</m>.
      The red curves show the decaying amplitude envelope <m>\pm e^{\alpha t} \cdot A</m>.
      Move the slider to change <m>\alpha</m>. At <m>\alpha = 0</m> the two graphs match.
    </caption>
    <interactive xml:id="interactive-underdamped-compare"
      iframe="ch3-graph/interactive-underdamped-compare.html"
      width="100%" aspect="5:3">
      <description>
        Two side by side graphs. The left graph shows x of t equals 3 sine of 2t plus
        cosine of 2t, oscillating with constant amplitude. The right graph shows
        e to the alpha t times the same expression, with a slider to control alpha
        between negative 0.5 and 0. As alpha becomes more negative the oscillations
        decay faster. At alpha equals 0 the right graph matches the left.
      </description>
    </interactive>
  </figure>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       REMARK: CONNECTION TO UNDAMPED, RAISES EQUILIBRIUM QUESTION
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  
    <p>
      The shape of the underdamped solution is very similar to the undamped case from
      <xref ref="sec-undamped-mass-spring"/>: both oscillate and cross the equilibrium
      position repeatedly. The key difference is the decaying amplitude, driven by the
      factor <m>e^{\alpha t}</m> with <m>\alpha \lt 0</m>. Physically, the dashpot is
      continuously removing energy from the system, causing the oscillations to shrink.
    </p>
    <p>
      This raises a natural question: does the mass keep oscillating back
      and forth across equilibrium, as in the undamped case? Or does it eventually settle
      on one side, either compressed or stretched?
      Explore the animation below to see if you can determine what happens!
    </p>


  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       INTERACTIVE 2: SPRING ANIMATION + x(t) GRAPH
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <figure xml:id="fig-underdamped-spring">
    <caption>
      The underdamped spring with <m>\alpha = -0.2</m> and <m>\omega = 3</m>.
      The position function is <m>x(t) = e^{-0.2t}(c_1 \cos(3t) + c_2 \sin(3t))</m>.
      Use the sliders to change <m>c_1</m> and <m>c_2</m> and press Play.
    </caption>
    <interactive xml:id="interactive-underdamped-spring"
      iframe="ch3-graph/interactive-underdamped-spring.html"
      width="100%" aspect="5:3">
      <description>
        On the left, an animation of a mass on a spring and dashpot system. On the right,
        the corresponding graph of x of t traced in real time. The decay rate alpha is
        fixed at negative 0.2 and omega is fixed at 3. Sliders control c1 and c2.
        As the animation plays the oscillations visibly shrink toward zero.
      </description>
    </interactive>
  </figure>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       MC CHECKPOINT
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <exercise xml:id="ex-mc-underdamped-crossings">
    <statement>
      <p>
        By observing the graph above, what can we deduce about how many times the mass
        passes through the equilibrium position <m>x = 0</m> as time goes on forever?
      </p>
    </statement>
    <choices randomize="no">
      <choice correct="yes">
        <statement><p>Infinitely many times.</p></statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Correct! The oscillations never fully stop in finite time. No matter how
            large <m>t</m> gets, the curve continues to cross <m>x = 0</m>. <m>\checkmark</m>
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement><p>Exactly once.</p></statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Look again at the graph. The curve crosses <m>x = 0</m> many times, not just once.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement><p>At most once.</p></statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            This is the behavior of overdamped and critically damped systems, not underdamped.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement><p>Never.</p></statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            The graph shows the curve oscillating back and forth across <m>x = 0</m> repeatedly.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
    </choices>
  </exercise>

 
    
    <p>
      Okay, so we now have an intuitive feel for how often it crosses the equilibrium position. However, to prove it without a doubt,
      we will go into the equation for the position function itself and show it algebraically.
    </p>   

  <example xml:id="ex-underdamped-crossings-algebra">
    <title>How Many Times Does the Mass Cross Equilibrium?</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        For an underdamped system, show algebraically that the mass passes through
        the equilibrium position <m>x = 0</m> infinitely many times.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        This is a homework problem. The argument is very similar to the one in
        <xref ref="ex-equil-crossings-algebra"/> for the undamped case, so reviewing
        that example first is a good starting point.
      </p>
      <p>
        <em>Hint:</em> start with <m>x(t) = 0</m> and use the fact that
        <m>e^{\alpha t} \neq 0</m> for all <m>t</m>.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </example>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       BRIDGE TO LIMIT: AMPLITUDE NEVER ZERO, BUT DOES IT VANISH?
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <p>
    So the mass keeps crossing equilibrium forever, no matter how small the oscillations
    get! This is because the trig functions make the position function oscillate as in
    the undamped case, and the decay factor <m>e^{\alpha t}</m> is never exactly zero.
    But the amplitude is still shrinking the whole time.
  </p>

  <p>
    Unlike the undamped case, the amplitude here is shrinking, so even though the mass
    keeps crossing equilibrium, it is also approaching equilibrium in the long run.
  </p>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       KEY TAKEAWAYS
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
  <observation xml:id="obs-underdamped-limit">
    <p>
      Even though the mass passes through equilibrium infinitely many times, the
      damping causes the oscillations to shrink down to zero:
      <me>
        \lim_{t \to \infty} x(t)
        = \lim_{t \to \infty}
          \underbrace{e^{\alpha t}}_{\to\, 0}
          \cdot
          \underbrace{\bigl(c_1 \cos(\omega t) + c_2 \sin(\omega t)\bigr)}_{\text{bounded}}
        = 0.
      </me>
      So the oscillations never fully stop in finite time, but they shrink to zero in
      the long run.
    </p>
  </observation>

  <conclusion>
    <title>Key Takeaways</title>
    <p>
      <ul>
        <li><p>
          <alert>Solution form:</alert> Underdamped solutions have the form
          <m>x(t) = e^{\alpha t}(c_1 \cos(\omega t) + c_2 \sin(\omega t))</m>
          with <m>\alpha \lt 0</m>.
        </p></li>
        <li><p>
          <alert>Decaying envelope:</alert> The factor <m>e^{\alpha t}</m> shrinks the
          amplitude of the oscillations toward zero. A more negative <m>\alpha</m> means
          faster decay.
        </p></li>
        <li><p>
          <alert>Equilibrium crossings:</alert> The mass passes through <m>x = 0</m>
          infinitely many times.
        </p></li>
        <li><p>
          <alert>Long-term behavior:</alert> <m>\displaystyle\lim_{t \to \infty} x(t) = 0</m>.
          The mass approaches equilibrium but oscillates forever while doing so.
        </p></li>
      </ul>
    </p>
  </conclusion>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       VIDEO + QUESTION FORM
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

